"\n\n<h3>I. The Residue Pushed to Center, Then Immediately Pulled Back</h3>\n\n<p>Lincoln had just recommitted the nation to the promise of equality and written abolition into the Constitution. Five days after Lee's surrender, a bullet transferred the presidency to a man who embodied the opposing impulse. The years from 1865 to 1868 are the story of a newly acknowledged debt meeting immediate resistance: who has the right to define the membership of the political community?</p>\n\n<p>The standard narrative frames these years as \"Southern restoration\" versus \"Radical Reconstruction.\" The more illuminating frame is this: the abolition of slavery created a question the constitutional order had never had to answer directly — who belongs to the political community as a full member? This question, long managed by being deferred to the margins, now had to be answered at the constitutional level. The Black Codes, the Freedmen's Bureau, the Civil Rights Act of 1866, the Fourteenth Amendment, the Reconstruction Acts, and the impeachment trial are not six separate events; they are one extended structural conflict about a single issue: who defines the community, and who gets to be in it?</p>\n\n<p>In the series' language: the deepest residue had just entered the core, and the old order's immediate response was to push it back out. Acknowledging a debt and paying a debt are different things. This essay traces what happened in the first years after the acknowledgment.</p>\n\n<h3>II. A Man Placed by War Coalition Politics</h3>\n\n<p>Andrew Johnson's political position requires understanding before assessing his choices. Born poor, nearly without formal education, a tailor by trade, he had risen through Tennessee politics as a Democrat who opposed secession — not out of antislavery conviction but because he identified with non-slaveholding white men against the planter aristocracy. In 1864, Lincoln ran on a National Union ticket rather than a purely Republican one, selecting Johnson as a running mate precisely because Johnson was the most prominent War Democrat in the country. Johnson became president not because he shared the Republican reconstruction program but because wartime coalition politics placed him on the ticket.</p>\n\n<p>Johnson's opposition to slavery was real but consistently entangled with racial hierarchy. He understood emancipation as a class matter — the overthrow of the planter elite — rather than as a rights matter. In his logic, abolishing slavery did not entail Black political equality. Existing voting rights belonged to white men; immediate Black political participation would trigger race war. His racial views appear directly in the record: in vetoing the Civil Rights Act he complained it discriminated against white people; his 1867 annual message denounced congressional Reconstruction as risking the \"Africanization\" of half the country. Contemporary historians who rank him at or near the bottom of the presidential rankings do so primarily not because he was politically inept — though he was — but because he refused after emancipation to acknowledge Black political equality as a proper component of the postwar order.</p>\n\n<p>The structural irony is sharp. The constitutional order had just, at enormous cost, moved its deepest residue to the center. A bullet made the new president someone who embodied the structural force most committed to pushing that residue back to the margins. Johnson was not an isolated bad actor; he represented a real and powerful social force — the old white republic seeking to perpetuate its dominance after the legal form of slavery had been destroyed. The accident was that this force happened to hold the presidency.</p>\n\n<h3>III. Presidential Reconstruction: Seizing the Power to Define</h3>\n\n<p>Between Lincoln's death and the convening of the Thirty-Ninth Congress in December 1865, Johnson operated through a constitutional gap. The Thirty-Eighth Congress had adjourned; the incoming Congress would not meet for months. In that interval, he effectively implemented Presidential Reconstruction unilaterally: issuing amnesty proclamations that restored property rights to most former Confederate participants, appointing provisional governors, and authorizing states to reconstitute their governments. His instructions for North Carolina, for example, limited participation in the new political order to those who had met the state's 1861 voter qualifications and taken a loyalty oath — a requirement that excluded the newly freed Black population entirely.</p>\n\n<p>The structural key is not the leniency itself but the question of who held the definitional power. Johnson positioned the president and Southern white elites as the primary definers of postwar political community, rather than the constitutionally elected body with the power to seat members and amend the Constitution. Congressional Republicans were angry not only at the pardons but at the result: Southern states were reconstituting governments that denied Black rights almost entirely, would restore full representation, and were sending former Confederate leaders back to Washington. When the Thirty-Ninth Congress convened, it refused to seat Southern representatives and formed the Joint Committee on Reconstruction — taking back the definitional power Johnson had seized in the interim.</p>\n\n<p>This episode illustrates a recurring feature of the constitutional order: its rules always leave some gaps, and in crisis moments those gaps can be exploited to pre-empt powers that properly belong to more representative institutions. The gap here was the months-long interval between Lincoln's death and a Congress capable of acting. Congress's response — using its hard constitutional powers over membership and amendment to reclaim the initiative — showed the order's self-correcting capacity, but only after significant damage had been done.</p>\n\n<h3>IV. The Black Codes: Compressing Freedom Back to Servitude</h3>\n\n<p>The most immediate and explosive consequence of Presidential Reconstruction was the enactment of Black Codes throughout the former Confederate states. Mississippi's code acknowledged that Black people could sue and hold personal property, then restricted them from renting land outside towns, required all free Black people to have documented legal employment or residence, made labor contracts of more than a month enforceable by forfeiture of wages earned and re-arrest by officials, and empowered courts to apprentice unsupported Black children — with preference given to their former masters — and to arrest adults without regular employment as vagrants. South Carolina's code called contracted Black workers \"servants\" and employers \"masters,\" permitted corporal punishment for disobedience, required licenses and fees for independent employment as artisans or tradespeople, and defined vagrancy so broadly as to capture nearly any Black person not in a plantation labor relation.</p>\n\n<p>The structural meaning of the Black Codes is this: the Thirteenth Amendment abolished slavery as a legal institution; the Codes immediately demonstrated that abolishing the institution had not resolved the underlying residue. The old order could no longer own people as property — that specific form had been foreclosed. But it could replicate the functional content of that ownership through contract law, vagrancy statutes, and licensing requirements. A person was nominally free and actually compelled. The residue had not been absorbed; it had changed form. The two-sidedness of the residue meant that closing off one path of domination caused the dominating force to find another. You cannot resolve an irresolvable residue by attacking any single institutional expression of it; the residue will reconstitute itself in a new form as long as its underlying source — the refusal to grant full political membership — remains intact.</p>\n\n<h3>V. The Freedmen's Bureau: The Gap Between Acknowledgment and Payment</h3>\n\n<p>The primary federal tool for managing the transition from slavery to citizenship was the Bureau of Refugees, Freedmen, and Abandoned Lands, established March 3, 1865. The Bureau distributed food, clothing, and medical care; established schools; supervised labor contracts; and handled land issues, legal disputes, and family reunification for formerly enslaved people. Its work established hundreds of schools that became the foundation of Black educational institutions in the South.</p>\n\n<p>But the Bureau operated under two permanent constraints: limited resources and enforcement capacity, and presidential hostility. Johnson vetoed the first extension bill in February 1866, calling it unnecessary. Congress revised and resubmitted; Johnson vetoed again in July. Both chambers overrode the second veto, and the Bureau continued operating for two more years. The fight over the Bureau was a core element of the larger Reconstruction conflict.</p>\n\n<p>The Freedmen's Bureau's historical significance in this series' framework is this: it was the constitutional order's first large-scale attempt not merely to acknowledge the deepest residue but to begin paying the debt — building the institutions that would allow formerly enslaved people to function as actual citizens. Its limits were equally revealing. Without land redistribution, without sufficient military force to protect against violence, and without presidential support, the Bureau could stabilize the transition but not complete it. Acknowledging a constitutional debt requires only text; paying that debt requires the state capacity to enforce it on the ground. The gap between the acknowledgment (Thirteenth Amendment) and the payment (actual equal citizenship in southern communities) was vast, and the Bureau's limits made that gap visible. The constitutional order had run far ahead of its enforcement capacity.</p>\n\n<h3>VI. The Civil Rights Act and the Fourteenth Amendment</h3>\n\n<p>Congress moved to secure the gains through legislation and then constitutional amendment. The Civil Rights Act of 1866 declared that all persons born in the United States and not subject to foreign powers — excluding non-taxed Indians — were citizens, and that citizens of every race and color were entitled to make and enforce contracts, sue, give evidence, inherit, and purchase, lease, and hold property, with full and equal benefit of all laws for security of person and property. Johnson vetoed it, complaining it favored Black over white Americans. The Senate overrode 33 to 15; the House 122 to 41. This was a watershed: Congress had publicly demonstrated, on a major piece of legislation, that the president was not the final arbiter of Reconstruction's direction.</p>\n\n<p>Recognizing that ordinary legislation could be repealed by a future Congress or challenged in court, Congress turned to constitutional amendment. The Fourteenth Amendment's provisions must be read section by section. Section 1 established birthright citizenship, prohibited states from abridging the privileges or immunities of citizens, prohibited deprivation of life, liberty, or property without due process, and required equal protection of the laws. Section 2 recalculated congressional representation based on total population but reduced representation proportionally for states that denied adult male citizens the right to vote — a transitional mechanism, not a direct mandate for Black male suffrage. Section 3 disqualified from federal and state office those who had violated an oath to the Constitution by participating in rebellion. Section 4 affirmed the federal debt and repudiated Confederate debts and claims for emancipated slaves.</p>\n\n<p>The Fourteenth Amendment's historical significance operates on multiple levels. It directly overturned Dred Scott's holding that Black people could not be federal citizens — a holding this series has tracked as an attempt to use judicial authority to permanently foreclose the deepest residue. The Constitution now answered that attempt with explicit inclusion. It placed equal citizenship and due process at the constitutional core rather than leaving them dependent on ordinary legislation. And its equal protection clause became, in later decades, the most generative provision in the American constitutional system.</p>\n\n<p>One feature must be noted without softening. Section 2, in apportioning the political cost of denying suffrage, introduced the word \"male\" explicitly into the Constitution for the first time. In the midst of the fiercest constitutional struggle over racial inclusion, the axis of sex-based exclusion was not merely left unaddressed — it was more explicitly encoded into the fundamental law. The Seneca Falls Declaration of 1848 had named this gap; the Fourteenth Amendment deepened it. Two axes of the same unfulfilled promise were moving in opposite directions simultaneously.</p>\n\n<h3>VII. The 1866 Elections and Radical Reconstruction</h3>\n\n<p>The 1866 midterm elections were the pivotal moment of Reconstruction's political history. Johnson campaigned against the Fourteenth Amendment and congressional leadership, undertaking a \"Swing Around the Circle\" tour of northern states that went badly — he was drawn into undignified public arguments with hecklers in Cleveland and other cities. Republicans won veto-proof supermajorities in both chambers.</p>\n\n<p>These results were not ordinary midterm fluctuations. They were a re-authorization by the electorate of congressional rather than presidential leadership on the central question of Reconstruction. In this series' terms: the constitutional order's mechanism for correcting presidential overreach — competitive elections — functioned exactly as designed. Johnson had used the presidential succession gap to pre-empt Congress's definitional role. The electorate returned a Congress capable of reclaiming it. The correction happened not through a coup or administrative reorganization but through the open verdict of voters.</p>\n\n<p>The Reconstruction Acts of 1867 followed. They declared the ex-Confederate states (except Tennessee, which had already ratified the Fourteenth Amendment) to lack lawful governments, divided them into five military districts, and established the conditions for restored representation: male citizens over 21, regardless of race or prior condition, would elect constitutional conventions; new state constitutions must guarantee this suffrage; and state legislatures must ratify the Fourteenth Amendment. The Reconstruction Acts explicitly elevated Black men from a labor-force management problem to a constitutive element of reconstruction legitimacy. Political membership and constitutional restoration were now linked.</p>\n\n<h3>VIII. Impeachment: When the Branches Fundamentally Disagree on Who Belongs</h3>\n\n<p>The impeachment of Andrew Johnson was the extreme institutional expression of the conflict over Reconstruction's direction. In March 1867, Congress overrode Johnson's veto of the Tenure of Office Act, which restricted the president's ability to remove high executive officers without Senate consent. Johnson suspended Secretary of War Stanton in August 1867 during a congressional recess; the Senate refused to concur in January 1868, restoring Stanton; Johnson dismissed Stanton again on February 21, 1868. The House voted to impeach 126 to 47 on February 24, producing eleven articles. The Senate trial ran from March through May 1868, and three critical votes each ended 35 to 19 — one vote short of the two-thirds required for conviction.</p>\n\n<p>Historians have offered two interpretations of the acquittal that are best held simultaneously rather than as alternatives. The first holds that the trial prevented Congress from converting impeachment into a tool for removing presidents on policy grounds, that the senators who voted against conviction were protecting the separation of powers and the independence of the executive, and that this preserved a constitutional norm of value beyond the immediate controversy. The second holds that focusing on the procedural principle obscures the substantive reality: the president acquitted was not an abstract official but one who had systematically obstructed Black citizenship, enabled the Black Codes, undermined congressional Reconstruction, and bought time for the old Southern order to reconstitute itself. Both interpretations capture something true.</p>\n\n<p>What the impeachment most clearly illuminates for this series is the constitutional order's limits when its two branches fundamentally disagree on the most basic political question — who belongs to the political community. When that question is what divides president and Congress, the constitutional text does not automatically produce a peaceful answer. It pushes the conflict to the institutional edge, as far as the rules permit, without resolving the underlying disagreement. The structure can provide a stage for the conflict to play out without becoming open violence; it cannot, by its procedural mechanism alone, settle the substantive question of membership. The deepest residue, even after it had been written into constitutional text, remained unresolved in the form of the question: what does membership actually mean in practice?</p>\n\n<h3>IX. Conclusion</h3>\n\n<p>The years of Johnson and Reconstruction illustrate a specific kind of constitutional dynamics: acknowledgment followed immediately by pushback. The Thirteenth Amendment acknowledged the debt; the Black Codes demonstrated that acknowledging it and paying it were entirely different things. The Fourteenth Amendment pushed the acknowledgment deeper into constitutional text; Johnson's vetoes and Presidential Reconstruction attempted to prevent the acknowledgment from having operational consequences.</p>\n\n<p>What the constitutional order demonstrated in these years was its capacity for self-correction through elections — not a guaranteed outcome but a structural possibility. Congress's Reconstruction legislation went as far as the veto-proof majority could push it. The electorate in 1866 produced that veto-proof majority. The order corrected its presidential imbalance through its open mechanisms, not through a coup. That is the non-closure dynamic visible even in this most contentious period.</p>\n\n<p>What the Black Codes demonstrated was the residue's form-changing persistence. Destroy slavery as an institution and the underlying claim — that Black people do not belong to the political community as equals — reconstitutes itself in a new form. The Freedmen's Bureau's limits revealed the gap between constitutional acknowledgment and actual state capacity to pay the debt on the ground. The Fourteenth Amendment's Section 2 showed that even the amendment addressing racial exclusion simultaneously deepened sex-based exclusion. The impeachment trial showed that when the two branches hold fundamentally incompatible views on membership, the constitutional structure cannot resolve the disagreement — it can only provide a venue where the disagreement plays out to its procedural limit.</p>\n\n<p>The rewriting was real. From the Thirteenth to the Fourteenth Amendment, the constitutional order moved the equality promise from deferred margin to constitutional core. But the gap between the text and the ground remained enormous, and the forces opposing the rewriting were not defeated — they were legislatively constrained. What followed was the test of whether those constraints could be enforced without the political will that had produced them, as casualties mounted, northern attention wavered, and the rewriting's opponents waited for the moment when the federal commitment would run out.</p>\n\n      "
